       "reasoning": "The task is to write a function that takes two lists and replaces the last element of the first list with the elements of the second list.\n\nLooking at the example:\n- First list: [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 10]\n- Second list: [2, 4, 6, 8]\n- Result: [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 2, 4, 6, 8]\n\nSo we take the first list, remove the last element (10), and then extend with the elements of the second list.\n\nThe function should:\n1. Take the first list and remove its last element\n2. Extend it with the elements of the second list\n\nLet me write this:\n\n```python\ndef replace_list(lst1, lst2):\n    lst1 = lst1[:-1] + lst2\n    return lst1\n```\n\nOr alternatively:\n```python\ndef replace_list(lst1, lst2):\n    lst1.pop()\n    lst1.extend(lst2)\n    return lst1\n```\n\nBoth should work. Let me verify with the example:\n- lst1 = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 10], lst2 = [2, 4, 6, 8]\n- lst1[:-1] = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]\n- [1, 3, 5, 7, 9] + [2, 4, 6, 8] = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 2, 4, 6, 8] \u2713\n\nI'll go with the first approach as it's cleaner and doesn't mutate the input.",
